// teach 5: add with !Alloc
// A lock is taught where something stands on it: `g` declares `!Alloc` and
// calls `f`, so `f` not allocating is what `g`'s clause rests on, and the
// lock moves the refusal of a future allocation in `f` to `f` itself.
fn f(x) = x + 1

fn g(x) with !Alloc = f(x)

fn main() = g(1)
